    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d628">

                <pb facs="0848" n="746" sameAs="frus:frus1935v01/pg746"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">765.84/1279</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Chief of the Division of Far Eastern
                        Affairs</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Hornbeck</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-09-12">September 12, 1935</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Mr. Secretary</hi>: In view of the strong position
                    which Sir Samuel Hoare took on behalf of the British Government in his address
                    at Geneva yesterday,<anchor n="71" xml:id="d628-f1"/> would not the present be a
                    particularly opportune moment for the American Government to give utterance to a
                    statement (such as we have been considering) indicative of our strong moral
                    support of the effort on behalf of peace which is being made by the British
                    Government and those other of the League powers who stand for that principle?
                    Would it not be highly desirable that this Government’s decision, one way or the
                    other, be arrived at, and if the decision be in the affirmative, action be
                    taken, today?</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>S[<hi rendition="#small-caps">tanley</hi>] K. H[<hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">ornbeck</hi>]</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
